# The Man Who Lost a Leg

Tamil title: காலிழந்தான்

"I am blind! I stumbled and fell," he said, and I could not suppress my laughter. I am not one whose heart remains unmoved by another's suffering, nor am I in the habit of mocking anyone when misfortune befalls them. Even so, I could not hear him without laughing.

I had never seen him before. He seemed new to the town. His appearance was dishevelled, his clothes dirty, and his gaze had a startled wildness.

There was a large crowd at the railway station that day. It had gathered to welcome my friend, and I too had gone there for that purpose. I did not know whether my friend expected me. A reception and a speech by him at the town hall had been arranged. I had gone only after seeing the announcement. I did not even know whether he had recognized me, but the moment I saw him I recognized him clearly. He had endured the battlefield, yet none of the old radiance had left his face.

I was delighted to see him. The organizers of the felicitation took my friend and his wife away in a motorcar. Modesty and beauty were joined in that woman.

The photographers and newspaper reporters walked along, talking and laughing.

"He fought with tremendous courage on the battlefield."

"He knew there was a land mine and tried to remove it."

"It was his bravery that saved many lives."

The more I heard them praise my friend in this way, the happier I became.

He had been courageous even in school. I remember it well. One day a fierce bull chased our teacher. The teacher ran screaming, and we ran too. It was my friend Kannappa who boldly seized the bull by its tail and pulled it, making it charge at him instead. The bull struck him hard.

"I am all right. I am young and can bear even severe pain. But the poor man is over fifty and has no strength. If that fierce bull had gored him and tossed him down, he would have been badly injured. He could not have borne it," Kannappa had said.

There were several pupils at school reputed to be rowdies, but none of them possessed that courage. It was Kannappa, known as the boy who never raised his bowed head, who showed such valour.

He had displayed the same courageous spirit on the battlefield. He deserved the praise; what doubt could there be?

I was walking along thinking of this when a man at the edge of the road caught his foot on something and nearly fell. He seemed to have been walking lost in thought. I caught him before he fell; otherwise he would have tumbled into the ten-foot ditch beside the road.

It was then that he muttered, "I am blind; I stumbled and fell." I could not contain my laughter. For although he called himself blind, he was a one-legged man: one leg had been amputated around the knee, and he could walk only by leaning on a stick and hobbling along.

There was nothing wrong with his eyes. It was his leg that was impaired, yet he had called himself blind. That was why I laughed. The very next moment, however, I felt ashamed. Poor man, what would he think of me? I apologized.

"Oh! You laughed because I should have said, 'I have lost a leg, so I stumbled,' and it sounded odd when I called myself blind? Never mind. I have lost a leg, and the whole town can see that. But even you cannot see that I have lost my sight. I myself discovered that I was blind only half an hour ago."

He spoke like this. I studied his eyes closely. They were uninjured and shining. He was not blind. Yet he insisted that he was. What mystery was this? I could not understand it. He hobbled beside me.

"Please do not be offended. Your eyes appear to be sound. Why do you reproach yourself as blind?" I asked respectfully.

"Perhaps you think I am mentally disturbed. My speech and conduct would naturally suggest it," he said. I understood that some great burden weighed on his heart and made him speak of himself with contempt. My compassion for him deepened.

"You may not have understood when I said that I discovered only half an hour ago that I was blind. You cannot understand without hearing my story. But you may have some business to attend to. Why should I trouble you? Let the story remain buried in my heart."

"I have no business. I have taken the day off. I am going straight to the town hall. Lieutenant Kannappa, who was just welcomed, is to speak there."

"Kannappa! So you came for the reception too, as I did. Do you know him?"

"Know him? We studied together at the same school. We are old friends."

"Is that so? Then perhaps it would not be proper for me to tell you. You wonder why? Do not be afraid. I am not mad or deranged; I merely lacked sense. In particular, I lacked the sense to understand women, to understand the character and greatness of the women of our land. Let that be. Did you speak to Lieutenant Kannappa just now?"

"No. I saw him only from a distance. I do not know whether he recognized me."

"Is that all? I thought you were close to each other.

"Like Lieutenant Kannappa, I too returned from the battlefield. I lost my leg there, just as Kannappa did, though he served on one front and I on another. We never met there.

"Thangam, his wife, is henceforth my sister. But before I went to war, I wished to marry her. She did not object when our parents agreed. We planned to marry before I left for the battlefield.

"One evening I went near the village pond at dusk. Two women carrying water pots were returning home. They did not seem to notice me, so absorbed were they in their conversation. They spoke loudly enough for me to hear. Since I had to go in the same direction, I was forced to hear them. Had I been deaf, I would not have become blind.

"'They say Thangam is to be married next week.'

"'Yes, they are giving her to a soldier.'

"'What a strange match! Thangam is such a timid girl, and the man she is to marry is a soldier. How will the two suit each other?'

"'Her stepmother is cruel, and Thangam is her husband's elder daughter. She must have decided that she would find peace only by throwing the girl into some ruined well, and so she has arranged this soldier.'

"'There is nothing contemptible about being a soldier. Going to war is an honour.'

"'That is not what I mean. Why this hurried wedding now? Let him finish his military service and return. It may take two years, but will the household drown before then? Why the haste?'

"'Why postpone an auspicious event?'

"'He is going to tie the marriage pendant around Thangam's neck and leave for the fighting. Who knows his fate? He may lose a leg or an arm, or even his life. If anything happens to him, will not Thangam have to spend the rest of her life with her head in her hands, weeping?'

"'So that is what you mean. It is true. They could wait a little.'

"'Should that not occur to the man himself? He is going where a war is raging. Who knows what may happen there? Why hurry to put the marriage pendant around a woman's neck? Once the war ends, if he returns alive and sound in limb and eye, they can think about the wedding then.'

"'He seems a senseless man.'

"'Perhaps he fears that someone else may marry Thangam.'

"'Certainly! As though a hundred men would come running to marry her!'

"'You mean because she is beautiful? Any man may desire her after seeing her beauty, but those who know that she is penniless will be afraid even to approach.'

"'Whatever the case, it is wrong for a soldier going to war to tie the marriage cord around a woman's neck and depart. He might as well put a noose around it.'

"I could bear no more. And the laughter with which those two women ended their conversation cut my heart to pieces. Were they not speaking the truth? My conscience tormented me. I feared that, without realizing it, I was about to do great harm. If anything happened to me on the battlefield, what would become of Thangam? How would her heart suffer? What would her stepmother say? 'I thought I could give her to somebody and be rid of her, but can that ever happen with her horoscope? This curse has come back to wind itself around my legs!' She was capable of saying anything that came to her tongue; I knew it myself.

"Thangam was dear to my heart, and I did not wish to lose her. But I also wished that no disgrace, blame, hardship, or grief should come to her because of me. I decided it was only right to marry after returning from the battlefield. Could I explain every reason in detail? I feared that people would invent arguments and change my mind.

"At first I thought we could hold the betrothal and postpone the wedding. It would assure me that Thangam was mine, while no one could accuse me of putting a noose around a woman's neck before going to war.

"On reflection, however, even that did not seem right. Who knew how long my return would take? Was Thangam to suffer under her stepmother until then? Her stepmother would revile her: 'Who knows when that worthy fellow will return? He has even held the betrothal and gone away, so I cannot give her to anyone else. She stands here like a milestone and torments me.' I abandoned that plan too.

"They pressed me to fix a date for the betrothal. I kept offering one excuse after another. Meanwhile, the day came for me to leave for the war front.

"I thought that if I could somehow meet Thangam alone, explain everything, and set her mind at rest, my own mind would be peaceful. But how? Her stepmother was hot-tempered and fiercely strict about propriety. If I mentioned my idea, she would flare up: 'Am I dead? Tell me whatever you have to say. What private talk do you have with her? This is not the place for that. Be off!' She would not stop with words; she would set the whole village in an uproar. Nor could I secretly meet Thangam. She was a modest girl who never lifted her bowed head and did not even come to the pond. What could I do?

"I sought out the astrologer.

"'I knew very well that when danger came you would seek me,' he began. After discoursing on every affair in the world, he finally agreed to help me. He would tell her stepmother that the planetary influences in my horoscope were unfavourable and that no betrothal should be held for another three months. Though he agreed to help me, he would not accept my reasons as reasons at all. He probed and questioned me.

"'Why, Somu? Is there some other woman?'

"'No, no! Nothing of the sort.'

"'Tell me if there is. It is only natural. The elders say desire lasts sixty days and infatuation thirty. Even in the age of the great men who mastered every scripture and were bound to truth, desire lasted only sixty days and infatuation thirty. These days it changes not by the hour but by the minute.'

"'Please do not think that of me.'

"'Ah, so you are a man of such resolve? The sage Viswamitra could close his eyes tightly and keep them shut for ten or sixteen years. But what became of him when Menaka arrived and danced?'

"What could I do? I had no one else to turn to, and I needed his help. I gritted my teeth and listened patiently.

"They trusted his warning and stopped the betrothal arrangements. But they promised that Thangam was mine and that they would seek no other match until I returned. I went to the front with that happiness in my heart.

"Though Thangam's family obeyed the astrologer, they were not fully satisfied. The astrologer also had a petty desire to have the village admire his cleverness. He told several people that the betrothal had been stopped because I had asked him to intervene tactfully.

"The report reached Thangam's household.

"I even heard that her stepmother said, 'Did I simply believe everything that fellow said? Do I not know how his tongue can bend either way? It is the girl's fate.'

"At the battlefront, an enemy attack trapped me in a tank. I should have died. Somehow the loss stopped with my leg.

"I did not even know that the leg had been amputated, for they had given me an anaesthetic. When I regained consciousness and opened my eyes, the pain and burning seemed to come from where the leg had been. I looked. My stomach lurched. 'Half my leg is gone! Oh!' I screamed. The cry exhausted me, and I fainted again.

"After I regained consciousness, I could not bear my grief. From then on I was a cripple! Yes, a man condemned to hobble. However unknown I might once have been, the whole town would now know me as 'Lame Somu.' If people were even slightly angry, they would not ask, 'Where is Somu?' They would ask, 'Where is that lame fellow?'

"Children would point me out to adults and say, 'Poor grandpa, look at the lame man!' Adults would tell children, 'Be careful on the road. Look for approaching vehicles. Otherwise, do you see that lame man? You will become like him.'

"Pity, ridicule, warning: these would arise wherever I moved.

"The world would no longer see my youth, strong body, or looks. It would see only that I had lost a leg.

"No one would speak of Somu as a good man, a soldier without misconduct, or a pleasant companion. The world would speak only of Somu as a cripple. It would not even say, 'Somu is lame,' but, 'Look, a lame man! His name is Somu.' I would no longer be Somu; I would be 'the lame man.'

"My heart suffered beyond measure. Grief tore it apart.

"But Thangam! The thought of her multiplied my sorrow a thousandfold and seized me with fear.

"The teasing words of the women by the pond had come true. Thangam's husband would be a man who had lost a leg. 'Thangam, must you marry a cripple? The world will mock you. How will you bear it?'

"'It was her fate to become the wife of a cripple.'

"'Poor woman, what suffering she must endure, married to him!'

"'Let a man be poor; let him carry burdens for a living. If only his limbs are sound, he can run about, labour, find a livelihood, and support a family.'

"'How can Thangam live peacefully with him? What can a man with one leg do? Who will employ him? The burden of supporting the family will fall on Thangam.'

"'She will also have to lead him everywhere and keep him from stumbling. All that work will fall on Thangam.'

"People would speak like this. How could Thangam bear it?

"Ruffianly boys would taunt my son: 'Go away, or I will break your leg as they broke your father's and hang it around your neck!'

"Why go so far? When seeking work, I myself would have to tell people, 'Sir, do not dismiss me because I have lost a leg. I cannot move about quickly, but I can work tirelessly while seated. I will do the work skilfully and conduct myself honestly.' If the employer were fond of cruel jokes and cared nothing for hurting others, he would say, 'You promise to conduct yourself honestly, but first you cannot even walk properly!'

"Everyone around him would have to laugh at the joke, and I would have to hear and endure it. How could I?

"Thangam would have to plead, 'My husband has lost a leg. He tried to earn a living by running a little shop from where he sat, but it did not succeed. That is why we could not repay your loan. Please give us a little more time.' I would have to live on, hobbling nearby and hearing her beg.

"When I imagined all this, I sometimes felt that the calamity had not stopped with my leg. I writhed like a worm in bed. The doctors thought I was convulsed with physical pain and administered one medicine after another, never knowing where the real pain lay.

"Thangam would have to accept all these troubles, humiliations, and taunts because of me.

"Yes, she was my Thangam, and only that made any of this possible. She was still unmarried and waiting for me. But now? What would happen when she learnt that I had lost a leg? Would she still say yes? Would she consent to marry me?

"How could a young woman bring herself to marry a cripple? Would it not be better for her to throw herself into a pond than spend her whole life suffering with one?

"What thoughts would pass through her mind? How bitterly would she cry?

"She would lament, 'Have you no eyes? Have you not even a grain of compassion? You are sacrificing me to a cripple! Is this justice? Would you do this to your own daughter?'

"Suppose they dragged her weeping into the wedding hall, seated her opposite me, and gave me the sacred cord to tie. My hand would tremble; her life would shudder; tears would spring to many eyes. The thought, 'The man about to tie the marriage pendant around my neck is a cripple,' would turn the ceremonial fire before us into a blaze consuming her heart.

"They would cry, 'Beat the wedding drums loudly!' The drums would thunder deafeningly. What would reach her ears: music, or the wail of death?

"People would say, 'Bridegroom, rise slowly, slowly.' Others would joke, 'The fellow is a soldier, after all. Strong and sturdy. So what if one leg is lame? He is capable; he will manage.' Or, 'In the excitement of the wedding, the bridegroom seems to have forgotten that he has only one leg. Look, he is ready to leap!'

"People without any malice would laugh at such jokes. I could not even be angry; though I felt no joy, I would have to bare my teeth in a smile. But what would Thangam think? What anguish would fill her heart?

"All that could happen only if Thangam agreed. How would she agree? However meek she was, she could not remain silent. She would refuse. Even her stepmother, cruel as she was, would not compel her in this matter, if only because taking a cripple into the house as a son-in-law promised no profit and only needless hardship and loss.

"Leaving all that aside, how could I myself ask them to arrange the wedding? Was I to say there was no difference between my former self and my present self except that half of one leg was missing? Was I some human beast?

"Could I hobble up to her and say that I had completed my duty on the battlefield and even returned with a medal? Had I lost my heart as well as my leg?

"If I stood before her in that state, would not her eyes spit fire? People would say, 'Look at the audacity of this lame fellow! He wants to marry Thangam. Would he agree to marry a blind woman? He claims there had already been an arrangement. What arrangement? Did he arrange to go to war, return with one leg, and marry her the moment he came back? Even if fate has dealt him this blow, should he not have enough sense to avoid bringing harm and disgrace upon another?'

"If he possessed such sense, they would call him virtuous and humane. Otherwise they would call him a beast, a madman, a lame wretch. What further abuse would be heaped upon him?

"Thoughts of every kind assailed me. One day I would resolve firmly that in my condition I must not marry Thangam. The next day longing would tear through me, and I would think, 'What does it matter? Perhaps they will agree if I ask.' A limb can be cut from the body and thrown away. Can a thought welling up in the heart be torn out and discarded? At most, one can keep it from rising again.

"The senior doctor gave me crutches and trained me to walk. 'There is no need for worry or fear now. Think of yourself as having the leg. You can walk well with crutches; you could even enter a running race,' he said to encourage me.

"Everyone praised him. They said he had saved my life by amputating the leg properly and at exactly the right time. Had he been even a little less skilful, poison from the decaying leg would have spread throughout my body and killed me. They thought I was now alive!

"I too praised and thanked him.

"'Your leg is gone, Mr Somu, and my reputation is made!' the doctor joked. Photographs were taken: the doctor alone, the doctor with me, me without the crutches, me with them, and many more. Were they not to be sent to Thangam?

"Quite by chance, the senior doctor cleared the confusion in my mind. After learning about my circumstances, he asked, 'Mr Somu, are you married?'

"'No, Doctor. I am not married.'

"He seemed greatly relieved. 'A good thing,' he said.

"That gave me clarity and resolve. The doctor must have been wondering what misery my wife would suffer now that I had lost a leg. He was relieved to hear that I was unmarried. The meaning was plain: in his opinion, a man in my condition should not marry.

"Was it only the doctor? Every generous-minded person would think the same. Was I so small-minded? My happiness was not what mattered. No woman should suffer humiliation or distress because of me. I would not permit it or earn that blame.

"Only after deciding that I would never go near her did I begin to sleep a little and feel hunger again. Those around me rejoiced that the patient had survived and recovered.

"I left military service; permission was easily granted. I wandered from place to place, trying to amuse myself so that grief could not approach me. I had never been a drinker, but I made intoxicants my new companion. Gambling, wandering, collapsing in a stupor: this became my way of life. I rapidly squandered everything I possessed, all to drown the grief that losing my leg had also made me lose Thangam.

"I cared nothing for the town, the world, or any public affair. I was a solitary man. I even pushed aside every thought of my family. Could a fallen flower be fastened back onto its plant? Many of my hopes had become fallen flowers; there was no place for them again in my heart.

"Sometimes I wished only to see how Thangam was. But I must not return there and create fresh complications. I decided not to go near the place until she had married and established a household. Only then might I visit.

"Last Tuesday, all the old thoughts I had buried sprang up and began to gnaw at my mind. By chance I saw an old newspaper. It carried a photograph of Thangam standing with her husband, Lieutenant Kannappa. Joy and bewilderment seized me together. Thangam was married! A lieutenant was her husband! What relief, what happiness!

"The caption said, 'See page six for details,' but page six was missing; the old newspaper was badly torn. I folded the fragment carefully and put it in my shirt pocket. I had never guarded anything with such care. I was consumed by a desire to learn the details. Neither drink nor gambling could restrain me. I searched out the newspapers and read the accounts with the hunger and haste of a man who finds food after four days without it. I learnt that they were arriving in this town today. I ran here and saw them to my heart's content."

I had forgotten myself in the emotion of Somu's story. What a good heart he possessed! He had shown such magnanimity to avoid blighting a woman's life.

"Somu, I wish to bow before you. You are a noble man! What a great sacrifice you made to avoid ruining a woman's life. Who else could possess such generosity?" I spoke with genuine feeling.

Somu stared fiercely at me.

"Somu, what I cannot understand is why a man of such purity condemned himself as blind," I said.

He looked me up and down.

"Sir, do you remember what I told you? I said that I was blind despite having eyes, that I took the wrong path and stumbled because I failed to understand the greatness of women's character. Do you remember?"

"I remember it clearly. But that is where you are wrong. You did not take the wrong path; you took the path of sacrifice. You did not stumble; you kept yourself from becoming an obstacle in a young woman's life."

"Because I had lost a leg, I thought it would be cruel to marry Thangam. I thought she would not consent and that, even if she did, it would be cruel of me to seek her. That is why I made no attempt to marry her, wandered the streets, and became what I am."

"You became a noble man. Only your body was diminished; your heart grew great."

"Did I try to learn what was in her heart?"

"That was the highest point of your sacrifice, the shining diamond in its crown."

"I never asked Thangam whether she would consent to marry me in my condition, and you call that the finest part of my sacrifice, a shining diamond! Sir, you are blinder than I am. I took another path because I assumed that marrying me would bring Thangam humiliation and blame, and that once she knew I had lost a leg she would refuse even to be asked. That is how I came to this state. Only half an hour ago did I realize that, though I had eyes, I had become blind by failing to understand the greatness of the women of our land. I sacrificed Thangam because I believed a man without a leg must not become her husband. But whom has she married? Lieutenant Kannappa, who, like me, lost part of a leg on the battlefield!"

I started in shock. Thangam had married a man who had lost a leg! Somu had sacrificed himself to keep her life from suffering, yet that sacrifice had proved meaningless: she had ultimately taken another one-legged man as her husband. Was Somu speaking in confusion, or was it true?

"What? Kannappa has lost a leg?" I asked, too ashamed now even to use the contemptuous label.

"You could not have known. Part of one of Kannappa's legs was amputated, just as mine was."

"But he walked in on two legs!"

"One is artificial. It has been made and fitted so skilfully by scientific methods."

"I could not detect it at all."

"Nor could I at first. He seems to have practised a great deal. When he walks, there is not the slightest sign of impairment. The reporters explained it all to me."

I could think of nothing to say. Somu continued while I stood stunned.

"I was wrong about Thangam. At the time, I failed to understand the distinctive nobility of our women. I had lost not merely my leg but my sight and judgement. I imagined that Thangam would hate and reject me when she learnt of my leg. Kannappa was not like that. His leg was gone, but his judgement remained clear. He neither concealed the truth nor feared or felt ashamed to state it. He did not deceive Thangam. Had he deceived her into marriage, I might even have become a murderer; anger is dangerous.

"Kannappa had sense. He frankly explained his condition and showed her the artificial leg science had given him. Thangam consented, and the marriage took place. I lacked that courage and candour. Cowardice overcame me. Afraid to face the truth, I ran and hid, and my whole form of life changed. Kannappa was brave. He faced the problem directly and obtained a good result.

"The moment I lost my leg, the fear that I would also lose my love overwhelmed me. Once I thought I could not have Thangam, life itself lost all colour. I refused every responsibility. I too knew that an artificial leg could be fitted, yet I made no attempt to obtain one. Why should I? Thangam would never be mine. What did it matter whether a cripple walked or crawled? My mind had grown that bitter.

"Kannappa was different. What should not have happened had happened; what could he do about it? Was he to think only of that and break his spirit? No. He chose to be content with what remained and to improve it as far as possible. He did not become a coward. He seized suffering by the neck, beat it back, drove it away, and triumphed."

"The reporters told you all these details?"

"Yes. Only then did I realize that I was blind. They are publishing a detailed article. Do you know something else? Merely remembering it makes all my anguish fly away and gives birth to a new joy. One reporter seems a rather bold fellow. He put any number of questions to Thangam. Do you know what he asked? He asked why she had consented to marry Lieutenant Kannappa after learning that he had lost a leg."

"What an insolent question!"

"I too would have thought so. It could have caused a quarrel. But do you know Thangam's answer?"

"Did she actually answer such a question?"

"I was the coward, sir. My sister Thangam is a woman of great courage. Is she not a lieutenant's wife? Do you know what she said? 'I am proud to take as my husband a man who lost his leg in defence of the nation.'"

He could speak no further, and neither could I. We walked together to the town hall. Somu seemed capable of walking even without his crutch; such new strength, awakening, enthusiasm, and pride had entered him.

14 January 1965

---NOTES---
The printed Tamil title is “காலிழந்தான்” (literally, “He Lost a Leg”). The filename transliteration appears irregular. The source repeatedly uses the historically common but now derogatory disability label “நொண்டி”; the translation uses contextual forms such as “one-legged man,” “lame,” and “cripple” where the characters’ stigma and self-directed contempt are integral to the story.
